S5  THE CLOSED-FORM INVERSION OF THE RC100 DARK-MATTER FRACTIONS
==============================================================================================================
  a0 = (1 - f_DM) g_obs / [ln(1/f_DM)]^2 for 99 of 100 galaxies (f_DM in (0.02, 0.98)); z = 0.61 - 2.52
  median a0 = 1.385e-10 m s^-2;  16-84%: 5.22e-11 - 3.46e-10;  median y = 1.45
  accelerations probed: y = g_bar/a0 16/50/84% = 0.33/1.45/5.30;  15 of 99 below 0.3;  A_obs there = 2.7/3.9/7.8
  d log10 a0/dz = -0.1123 +/- 0.0626 (bootstrap);  constant: 0 (-1.8 sigma);  halo-emergent mean slope +0.1311 (-3.9 sigma);  H(z) mean slope +0.2304 (-5.5 sigma)
  what drives it: d log10 f_DM/dz = -0.140  (the published fall of the dark-matter fraction with redshift); the inversion is monotone in f_DM
  scatter about the fit: 0.38 dex;  corr(log a0_hat, log y_hat) = -0.73  (noise in f_DM moves a0_hat and y_hat oppositely)
  controlling for log g_obs (measured, a0-free):   d log a0/dz = -0.138 +/- 0.063   -> halo-emergent mean slope disfavoured at 4.3 sigma
  controlling for log y_hat (absorbs noise AND part of any real signal): +0.008 +/- 0.048   -> 2.6 sigma
  weakest exclusion of the H(z) mean slope over the three treatments: 4.7 sigma
  range of the redshift slope over the three treatments: -0.14 to +0.01;  weakest exclusion of the halo-emergent slope: 2.6 sigma
  baryonic-mass calibration drift beta [dex per unit z]  ->  slope, and its distance from constant / from the halo law:
     beta -0.100: N  92, slope +0.159 +/- 0.069;  constant +2.3 sigma;  halo law -0.4 sigma
     beta -0.075: N  93, slope +0.089 +/- 0.061;  constant +1.5 sigma;  halo law +0.7 sigma
     beta -0.050: N  94, slope +0.021 +/- 0.062;  constant +0.3 sigma;  halo law +1.8 sigma
     beta -0.025: N  97, slope -0.044 +/- 0.058;  constant -0.7 sigma;  halo law +3.0 sigma
     beta +0.000: N  99, slope -0.112 +/- 0.063;  constant -1.8 sigma;  halo law +3.9 sigma
     beta +0.025: N  97, slope -0.182 +/- 0.072;  constant -2.5 sigma;  halo law +4.3 sigma
     beta +0.050: N  90, slope -0.148 +/- 0.073;  constant -2.0 sigma;  halo law +3.8 sigma
  admitting the one galaxy at the f_DM = 0.02 edge: slope -0.112 -> -0.085 (N 100), a 0.4 sigma move from one object
  the halo law comes within 2 sigma at beta = -0.05; constancy goes 2 sigma off at beta = -0.1
  KMOS3D replication (L332): T1 FAILED (framework max |z| 5.4; LCDM min |z|: NFW 6.1, H 6.3, C 6.2, K 6.2, A 6.2, ALL 6.1);  K1: slope <= -0.110; below-baryons fraction z>1.9 0.27-0.54 vs z<1.2 <= 0.11; worst Fisher p 2.6e-04
  injection (kernel-consistent f_DM with 0.05 noise on the real z, g_bar): injected -> recovered slope: +0.000 -> -0.000, +0.131 -> +0.130, +0.230 -> +0.229
  [PASS] [identity] S5a the inversion is exact: nu(y) (1 - f_DM) = 1 at y = [ln(1/f_DM)]^2
  [PASS] [data] S5e AGAINST INTEREST: a baryonic-mass calibration drift of at most 0.05 dex per unit z brings the halo law within 2 sigma (the result is calibration-conditional)   (beta = -0.05)
  [PASS] [data] S5f AGAINST INTEREST: the independent KMOS3D replication (L332) failed, and a third to a half of its z > 1.9 galaxies rotate below their own Newtonian baryons   (slope <= -0.110; below-baryons fraction z>1.9 0.27-0.54 vs z<1.2 <= 0.11; worst )
  [PASS] [identity] S5g the tilt table at beta = 0 reproduces the main fit exactly (same galaxies, same slope)
  [PASS] [injection] I5 the inversion recovers injected trends (0, halo law, H(z)) to 0.02 dex per unit z when f_DM obeys the kernel   (+0.000->-0.000, +0.131->+0.130, +0.230->+0.229)
  [PASS] [data] S5b the RC100 trend is consistent with constant a0 within 2.5 sigma and more than 3 sigma below the halo-emergent mean slope   (-0.112 +/- 0.063)
  [PASS] [data] S5c no decline is claimed: the slope is within 2.5 sigma of zero under every treatment
  [PASS] [data] S5d AGAINST INTEREST: the 3.9 sigma does not survive every control -- the weakest exclusion of the halo-emergent slope is below 3 sigma, and that is the number to quote   (weakest 2.6 sigma)

